00:03:56.640 --> 00:03:59.240
I try to keep this pretty simple.

72
00:03:59.240 --> 00:04:03.440
So ultimately, the sort of the end game of a software factory is one

73
00:04:03.440 --> 00:04:06.760
in which you have an agent system, a series of

74
00:04:07.280 --> 00:04:11.360
loops that build the entire end product that you ship

75
00:04:11.360 --> 00:04:16.320
to users, customers, anything like that, and where humans are doing

76
00:04:16.440 --> 00:04:20.400
all of their work shaping tickets, shaping tasks for the factory,

77
00:04:20.400 --> 00:04:24.280
and then working to improve the autonomy,

78
00:04:24.400 --> 00:04:27.680
automation and quality of the system.

79
00:04:27.680 --> 00:04:30.240
So think of it as we're monitoring the processes.

80
00:04:30.240 --> 00:04:33.240
The processes are building the widgets.

81
00:04:34.000 --> 00:04:38.320
You may ask, what do you mean autonomy, automation, quality, etc..

82
00:04:38.680 --> 00:04:42.560
So first, to give you all a framework, Tassell ourselves,

83
00:04:42.840 --> 00:04:43.880
we dogfood our own product.

84
00:04:43.880 --> 00:04:47.360
We are developing, I'd say predominantly via a software factory.

85
00:04:47.360 --> 00:04:50.440
At this point, what we have learned is that there are three

86
00:04:50.440 --> 00:04:53.880
big dimensions that you want to track in your

87
00:04:54.840 --> 00:04:57.160
rollouts of agents before

88
00:04:57.160 --> 00:05:00.280
software factories, but then as you move into software factories, for sure.

89
00:05:00.920 --> 00:05:04.720
The first is autonomy, which you can think of as how many course

90
00:05:04.720 --> 00:05:08.480
corrections do you have to give an agent to get to the right answer?

91
00:05:09.000 --> 00:05:09.720
You can.

92
00:05:09.720 --> 00:05:11.840
This one can be very easily monitored, right.

93
00:05:11.840 --> 00:05:14.200
It's like literally how many human turns did it take?

94
00:05:14.200 --> 00:05:17.720
Or if it puts up a PR, how many comments were needed on the PR?

95
00:05:18.120 --> 00:05:19.600
This is a metric that you're going to want to track.

96
00:05:19.600 --> 00:05:20.960
You're going to want to drive down.

97
00:05:20.960 --> 00:05:26.040
You're going to sort of go in this in this order as you build.

98
00:05:26.880 --> 00:05:29.680
Automation is related but not the same.

99
00:05:29.680 --> 00:05:34.240
So automation is how much oversight do agents need.

100
00:05:34.720 --> 00:05:35.640
So you could have a world.

101
00:05:35.640 --> 00:05:37.840
You can have a world where agents are actually very autonomous.

102
00:05:37.840 --> 00:05:40.440
You don't actually have to course correct them,

103
00:05:40.440 --> 00:05:44.120
but they're not automated because you don't trust them yet.

104
00:05:44.160 --> 00:05:45.960
So you're still reviewing all of their work,

105
00:05:45.960 --> 00:05:49.360
you're still checking their shoulder, you're just not making any corrections.

106
00:05:49.400 --> 00:05:53.800
And that's actually one of the major ruts we see people get into is

107
00:05:53.880 --> 00:05:55.720
they could be delegating a lot more to agents,

108
00:05:55.720 --> 00:05:59.400
but for a variety of reasons that are not yet able to.

109
00:05:59.440 --> 00:06:02.920
And so they end up getting review as a bottleneck is one of the first things

110
00:06:02.920 --> 00:06:03.800
you'll hear

111
00:06:03.800 --> 00:06:08.320
as people really have unlocked autonomy but have not unlocked automation.

112
00:06:08.800 --> 00:06:10.920
And then finally, quality. This is the easy one.

113
00:06:10.920 --> 00:06:13.080
This is just like, is your product still good?

114
00:06:13.080 --> 00:06:16.800
As you start to ship more with agents you don't want to be shipping slop.

115
00:06:17.640 --> 00:06:19.920
At Tessl, we believe that actually software

116
00:06:19.920 --> 00:06:24.480
factories and agentic coding is a gateway to much higher quality

117
00:06:24.480 --> 00:06:28.560
in the software that you produce, which I'll talk about in a minute.

118
00:06:28.840 --> 00:06:31.120
And by a minute I mean right now.

119
00:06:31.120 --> 00:06:35.560
So thinking about a software factory like you are

120
00:06:36.240 --> 00:06:39.440
working through these metrics, you're trying to delegate more to an agent.

121
00:06:39.840 --> 00:06:41.320
Why would you want to?

122
00:06:41.320 --> 00:06:44.320
Why would you think that that is a smart thing to do.

123
00:06:44.320 --> 00:06:46.400
Besides, you want your stock price to go up

124
00:06:46.400 --> 00:06:50.680
because you said you have an AI strategy first. Yes.

125
00:06:50.720 --> 00:06:53.160
What most people talk about higher velocity.

126
00:06:53.160 --> 00:06:55.720
I think this is where most people start.

127
00:06:55.720 --> 00:06:57.560
This will pay out pretty quickly.

128
00:06:57.560 --> 00:07:01.520
Like the minute you unlock a good amount of autonomy in your system,

129
00:07:01.520 --> 00:07:03.480
you will find that it is faster

130
00:07:03.480 --> 00:07:07.080
to write code through agents than it is to type the code yourself.

131
00:07:07.480 --> 00:07:10.840
But the big one, and I think this is when we we're

132
00:07:10.840 --> 00:07:14.080
in a bit of a transition period, is unlocking

133
00:07:14.120 --> 00:07:17.560
actually higher quality to the code that you ship as well.

134
00:07:17.560 --> 00:07:21.760
And so right now there's this very big trade off people talk about like,

135
00:07:21.800 --> 00:07:24.400
oh yeah, you can go faster, but aren't you shipping slop?

136
00:07:24.400 --> 00:07:27.000
Or the agents are writing weird code.

137
00:07:27.000 --> 00:07:28.120
Some of that is definitely true.

138
00:07:28.120 --> 00:07:31.560
And we'll talk about how you can mitigate that with harness engineering.

139
00:07:32.000 --> 00:07:33.640
I want to take a step back.

140
00:07:33.640 --> 00:07:38.440
Let's look maybe six months out, maybe six weeks out.

141
00:07:38.480 --> 00:07:41.360
Who knows the rate of things.

142
00:07:41.360 --> 00:07:45.560
There are two big things, in my opinion, and my years of software

143
00:07:45.560 --> 00:07:49.320
engineering that lead to low quality software.

144
00:07:49.440 --> 00:07:52.080
The first is just incomplete.

145
00:07:52.080 --> 00:07:55.120
Things get through review because people are either moving quickly, or

146
00:07:55.600 --> 00:07:58.720
they themselves are not versed in the system that they are reviewing.

147
00:07:58.960 --> 00:08:01.480
They look with a certain bias.

148
00:08:01.480 --> 00:08:05.040
And so this is an area where coding agents, when you sort of

149
00:08:05.080 --> 00:08:08.400
unlocked a good agent code review process,

150
00:08:08.960 --> 00:08:11.080
they're like the ultimate code reviewer, right?

151
00:08:11.080 --> 00:08:12.080
They're tireless.

152
00:08:12.080 --> 00:08:13.120
They'll check everything.

153
00:08:13.120 --> 00:08:16.560
You can have 15 of them checking every lens very specifically

154
00:08:16.600 --> 00:08:20.320
like security, reuse, usability, etc.

155
00:08:20.760 --> 00:08:25.080
but also you can be running like an agentic QA.

156
00:08:25.160 --> 00:08:28.000
You can be having an agent poke around with your like,

157
00:08:28.000 --> 00:08:31.720
the amount of energy and tokens that you can devote to

158
00:08:31.760 --> 00:08:35.880
review is no longer capped by like a human bashing their head against the wall.

159
00:08:35.920 --> 00:08:37.680
Like I'm tired of reviewing code.

160
00:08:37.680 --> 00:08:39.840
And so there is, I think, as a starting point,

161
00:08:39.840 --> 00:08:40.840
and we're starting to see this

162
00:08:40.840 --> 00:08:46.040
with companies that are towards the front end of a agentic adoption, companies

163
00:08:46.040 --> 00:08:49.440
like intercom, where they're actually noticing a decrease

164
00:08:49.440 --> 00:08:52.680
in bugs that get through review because they have scaled up

165
00:08:52.680 --> 00:08:55.920
how much effort they're putting into their review process.

166
00:08:56.720 --> 00:08:59.400
The second one, though, is that actually,

167
00:08:59.400 --> 00:09:02.640
in my experience, most bugs in software

168
00:09:02.720 --> 00:09:06.600
or known about and they are just P2 on a backlog somewhere.

169
00:09:06.800 --> 00:09:09.560
We just don't have time to fix it. It's not a priority.

170
00:09:09.560 --> 00:09:10.880
User flow.

171
00:09:10.880 --> 00:09:14.360
Oftentimes we knew the bug and we shipped with it anyways

172
00:09:14.360 --> 00:09:16.000
because it was too expensive to fix.

173
00:09:16.000 --> 00:09:19.960
And so one of, in my opinion, the most exciting things about

174
00:09:20.080 --> 00:09:22.320
as you are able to delegate more work to agents

175
00:09:22.320 --> 00:09:25.360
is that the idea of a backlog kind of goes away.

176
00:09:25.400 --> 00:09:28.600
Not entirely, but your capacity is so much larger.

177
00:09:28.840 --> 00:09:32.840
Your ability to just fix all the issues you find goes way, way up.

178
00:09:32.840 --> 00:09:36.760
And so I think as we get through some of the teething pains of working on

179
00:09:36.760 --> 00:09:37.480
software factories,

180
00:09:37.480 --> 00:09:40.680
we're actually going to see a world where software is much better.

181
00:09:40.680 --> 00:09:44.000
And in particular, it's much better for niche use cases, like people who

182
00:09:44.040 --> 00:09:47.840
otherwise would not have been a priority to fix their issues in the past.

183
00:09:49.360 --> 00:09:50.680
Third, a software factory

184
00:09:50.680 --> 00:09:55.080
because by definition, you're not gate kept on who can write code anymore.

185
00:09:55.120 --> 00:09:56.880
You can do broader exploration.

186
00:09:56.880 --> 00:10:00.200
You can bring in people from your GTM teams from design.

187
00:10:00.480 --> 00:10:02.040
We had somebody from our people ops

