00:13:04.640 --> 00:13:06.320
against the results.

245
00:13:06.320 --> 00:13:09.920
So if you've ever told your agent to use red, green, TDD,

246
00:13:10.000 --> 00:13:13.000
anything like that, you've been working on the inner loop.

247
00:13:13.800 --> 00:13:17.000
The biggest difference between like the inner loop for yourself

248
00:13:17.000 --> 00:13:21.440
and the inner loop for an agent is that now unit tests and linters,

249
00:13:21.440 --> 00:13:23.960
you don't have to write them, you don't have to maintain them.

250
00:13:23.960 --> 00:13:27.920
And so this is I think one of the first unlocks is to realize

251
00:13:28.000 --> 00:13:30.760
you can be so much more annoying with your inner loop.

252
00:13:30.760 --> 00:13:35.760
Now, like Max pedantic on the linting, you can have specific rules

253
00:13:35.760 --> 00:13:39.000
that check that certain modules don't import other modules.

254
00:13:39.160 --> 00:13:41.600
The kind of stuff you would have told yourself in the past,

255
00:13:41.600 --> 00:13:43.000
this is going to bit rot.

256
00:13:43.000 --> 00:13:44.000
Don't do this.

257
00:13:44.000 --> 00:13:46.760
Everyone hates this now.

258
00:13:46.760 --> 00:13:50.560
It's like the minute it fails and agent sees it, realizes it, fixes it.

259
00:13:50.560 --> 00:13:53.320
And so just basically every time you see an agent make a mistake,

260
00:13:53.320 --> 00:13:54.560
you should be thinking,

261
00:13:54.560 --> 00:13:58.360
how do I put this into a check so that it never happens again?

262
00:13:58.400 --> 00:13:58.600
Right.

263
00:13:58.600 --> 00:14:02.480
And you're going to end up with just massive linting, massive test suites.

264
00:14:02.720 --> 00:14:05.520
And over time you'll want to strip some of them out.

265
00:14:05.520 --> 00:14:08.400
So you want a process to sort of reevaluate if you need them.

266
00:14:08.400 --> 00:14:09.680
But I think this is like

267
00:14:09.680 --> 00:14:14.080
a big change in how we view the tests and the linting that we we write.

268
00:14:15.520 --> 00:14:17.920
Also, how easy is your product to use by an agent?

269
00:14:17.920 --> 00:14:19.000
Is another one here right.

270
00:14:19.000 --> 00:14:22.120
Can it access it via CLI or things like that.

271
00:14:23.640 --> 00:14:26.560
The outer loop is what builds automation.

272
00:14:26.560 --> 00:14:30.280
So this is ideally a set of slower, more in-depth checks

273
00:14:30.280 --> 00:14:34.200
that build up your confidence in this

274
00:14:34.240 --> 00:14:37.040
PR is correct so that you have to spend less time

275
00:14:37.040 --> 00:14:39.040
actually reviewing every piece of the code.

276
00:14:39.040 --> 00:14:44.160
And so the bread and butter starting point for this is an agentic code review.

277
00:14:44.280 --> 00:14:46.680
You want as much as you can.

278
00:14:46.680 --> 00:14:51.040
I think Claude code does the estimate like $25 for a PR review.

279
00:14:51.200 --> 00:14:52.120
That sounds crazy.

280
00:14:52.120 --> 00:14:54.720
But then also you think like the time a human would spend.

281
00:14:54.720 --> 00:14:57.520
Maybe it's not as crazy at Tessl.

282
00:14:57.520 --> 00:15:00.040
We think you can get it a lot cheaper, so don't worry $25.

283
00:15:00.040 --> 00:15:04.280
It's not the going rate of PR review, but you get a sense

284
00:15:04.280 --> 00:15:07.000
that these things are more expensive, they're more involved.

285
00:15:07.000 --> 00:15:11.960
A lot of what we've found that works really well here is actually pulling down

286
00:15:11.960 --> 00:15:16.200
into like a into a box, build your product, run it, have the agent

287
00:15:16.200 --> 00:15:18.160
click through, take screenshots,

288
00:15:18.160 --> 00:15:20.360
show you the thing in action so that you can review that.

289
00:15:20.360 --> 00:15:23.040
I think Cursor has a tool to do this as well.

290
00:15:23.040 --> 00:15:28.200
Anything you can do that doesn't make sense to run as the agent builds,

291
00:15:28.200 --> 00:15:32.240
but that you would want to look at to give you confidence that the thing works.

292
00:15:32.240 --> 00:15:34.200
This is your outer loop.

293
00:15:35.800 --> 00:15:37.480
Then finally, the meta loop

294
00:15:37.480 --> 00:15:41.400
is basically everything I just said on autopilot.

295
00:15:41.400 --> 00:15:43.960
And so you will start with the inner loop.

296
00:15:43.960 --> 00:15:45.120
You will build that up.

297
00:15:45.120 --> 00:15:49.440
Then you will move to getting your code review, getting your agent QA,

298
00:15:49.520 --> 00:15:51.600
all of that nice and tidy.

299
00:15:51.600 --> 00:15:55.560
And then you're going to get really, really tired of doing all of that.

300
00:15:55.560 --> 00:15:59.240
And you will start setting up loops of agents that watch it for you.

301
00:15:59.240 --> 00:16:02.360
So you'll have an agent that looks at all the CI checks

302
00:16:02.360 --> 00:16:06.160
from the last week and says, hey, here are three mistakes that kept getting made.

303
00:16:06.160 --> 00:16:09.400
I'm going to add linting rules for them so they don't make it to CI.

304
00:16:09.400 --> 00:16:13.480
Or oh, I noticed that agents made these three mistakes on

305
00:16:13.520 --> 00:16:17.640
like there were comments on the PRs that told the agent, don't do this.

306
00:16:17.960 --> 00:16:22.360
Let me pull back, let me add a skill or let me add unit tests in this area.

307
00:16:22.560 --> 00:16:25.000
And so the meta loop is sort of what feeds back in.

308
00:16:25.000 --> 00:16:28.600
And the meta loop is ultimately where you'll spend most of your time is creating

309
00:16:28.600 --> 00:16:33.360
these sort of like maintenance agents that watch everything else and make changes

310
00:16:33.560 --> 00:16:36.960
that you either review or don't review, depending on your sort of comfort level,

311
00:16:37.000 --> 00:16:40.160
the importance of the part of the tech stack, etc..

312
00:16:42.200 --> 00:16:42.520
Okay,

313
00:16:42.520 --> 00:16:45.960
so hopefully what I just described doesn't seem that complicated, right?

314
00:16:46.000 --> 00:16:49.240
It's mostly have good unit tests, have good linting,

315
00:16:49.400 --> 00:16:53.040
maybe be a little bit more persnickety about what you put into those suites.

316
00:16:53.040 --> 00:16:56.640
And then it's just a lot of like you have a problem, fix it with agents.

317
00:16:56.680 --> 00:16:56.920
Right?

318
00:16:56.920 --> 00:16:59.920
Like the that loop is pretty straightforward.

319
00:17:00.280 --> 00:17:02.480
So why is harness engineering hard.

320
00:17:02.480 --> 00:17:05.240
And the answer is almost exclusively organizational.

321
00:17:05.240 --> 00:17:08.160
It is just a different way of working for a lot of people.

322
00:17:08.160 --> 00:17:09.760
It's not a fun way of working.

323
00:17:09.760 --> 00:17:12.120
And so we'll talk about how to make it fun.

324
00:17:12.120 --> 00:17:14.240
But let's walk through a few of them.

325
00:17:14.240 --> 00:17:17.520
So the first is that knowing how to write a good agent, knowing

326
00:17:17.560 --> 00:17:22.440
when it's time to embrace a new technique versus not, there's just like an ever.

327
00:17:22.480 --> 00:17:25.840
Like I said, the name of this talk has changed four times in two weeks.

328
00:17:25.840 --> 00:17:27.840
The knowledge is constantly changing.

329
00:17:27.840 --> 00:17:30.800
It's annoying to keep up with it. That's not your day job.

330
00:17:30.800 --> 00:17:33.880
You want to focus on shipping features.

331
00:17:34.560 --> 00:17:37.680
Part of that harness engineering often feels like

332
00:17:37.720 --> 00:17:41.160
unplanned work, so you're trying to build a thing.

333
00:17:41.200 --> 00:17:44.160
You file a ticket, the agent picks it up, it starts working.

334
00:17:44.160 --> 00:17:45.880
It breaks.

335
00:17:45.880 --> 00:17:49.240
The answer in harness engineering is don't tell the agent to fix it.

336
00:17:49.280 --> 00:17:50.640
Instead, stop.

337
00:17:50.640 --> 00:17:51.920
Throw that PR away.

338
00:17:51.920 --> 00:17:53.680
Go back and think like what could have made it

339
00:17:53.680 --> 00:17:56.160
so the agent wouldn't have made this mistake.

340
00:17:56.160 --> 00:17:57.640
It's going to be a unit test.

341
00:17:57.640 --> 00:18:00.200
It's going to be a skill. It's going to be something like that.

342
00:18:00.200 --> 00:18:02.760
Make that change and then roll the dice again.

343
00:18:02.760 --> 00:18:04.080
And hopefully this time it works.

344
00:18:04.080 --> 00:18:06.840
And if it doesn't, throw that PR away and do another one,

345
00:18:06.840 --> 00:18:10.040
and nobody likes working that way, it's like a slot machine.

346
00:18:10.040 --> 00:18:12.440
It's the worst kind of slot machine.

347
00:18:12.440 --> 00:18:13.560
As part of that.

348
00:18:13.560 --> 00:18:15.760
Then if you are doing this work,

349
00:18:15.760 --> 00:18:18.240
let's say eventually you got to a place where it shipped.

350
00:18:18.240 --> 00:18:21.160
You could have shipped two hours ago if you would have just corrected it.

351
00:18:21.160 --> 00:18:21.320
Right.

352
00:18:21.320 --> 00:18:25.000
And so it's really hard to make the case for no,

353
00:18:25.040 --> 00:18:29.040
let me go do some science instead of launching the feature to customers.

354
00:18:29.040 --> 00:18:32.240
And it's very hard to make that argument to your boss

355
00:18:32.240 --> 00:18:35.600
or to your customers, things like that.

356
00:18:35.760 --> 00:18:39.080
And then finally, just really bad legibility.

357
00:18:39.080 --> 00:18:41.360
Like people will start working with Claude Code.

358
00:18:41.360 --> 00:18:42.520
They're making all these mistakes.

359
00:18:42.520 --> 00:18:43.120
You don't realize

360
00:18:43.120 --> 00:18:46.240
you're all making the same mistake or hitting the same pain points.

361
00:18:46.320 --> 00:18:47.640
And so it's hard to fix it.

362
00:18:47.640 --> 00:18:49.040
It's hard to know that, hey, aren't

363
00:18:49.040 --> 00:18:52.040
we all having a problem with this one area of the code base?

364
00:18:52.040 --> 00:18:53.640
It comes out slowly of people

365
00:18:53.640 --> 00:18:57.320
talking about it, or you realizing certain areas are harder to work in.

366
00:18:57.360 --> 00:18:59.480
It's just hard to fix things quickly.

367
00:19:01.320 --> 00:19:02.760
So there's going to be some pieces

368
00:19:02.760 --> 00:19:05.880
that you want to pull together to make that easier.

369
00:19:05.880 --> 00:19:07.680
And then also I'll talk about

370
00:19:07.680 --> 00:19:11.600
sort of Tessl's design philosophy is to try and make harness

371
00:19:11.600 --> 00:19:15.480
engineering fun and seamless rather than organizational change.

372
00:19:15.480 --> 00:19:19.520
So what are the components that you need to do?

373
00:19:19.600 --> 00:19:20.680
Everything I just described,

374
00:19:20.680 --> 00:19:24.160
but in a way that feels less ad hoc, that feels less painful.

375
00:19:25.520 --> 00:19:27.640
The first, and this is if I had to give like

376
00:19:27.640 --> 00:19:32.280
one piece of advice to anyone, I would start here of like,

377
00:19:32.320 --> 00:19:33.240
you want to level up

378
00:19:33.240 --> 00:19:36.280
how you're using coding agents, just do like this one simple trick.

379
00:19:36.360 --> 00:19:37.240
It's actually not that.

380
00:19:37.240 --> 00:19:39.000
It's like there's a little bit of work to it.

381
00:19:39.000 --> 00:19:41.600
Change how you work with the agents.

382
00:19:41.600 --> 00:19:45.720
So even if you are using Claude Code, codex, whatever

383
00:19:45.720 --> 00:19:47.520
and one shotting everything and you think like,

384
00:19:47.520 --> 00:19:50.920
oh yeah, it's easy, I just paste my prompt in my tab away

385
00:19:50.920 --> 00:19:55.920
I go to something else, it puts up the PR, and then I just go work in the PR

386
00:19:55.920 --> 00:19:59.360
and then I correct it in the session and then keep going.

387
00:20:00.800 --> 00:20:01.840
You like that?

388
00:20:01.840 --> 00:20:04.880
Feels almost the same as I file a ticket.

389
00:20:05.040 --> 00:20:06.520
It works in the background.

390
00:20:06.520 --> 00:20:09.040
It puts up a PR, I leave comments on the PR,

391
00:20:09.040 --> 00:20:12.040
it automatically picks up and makes changes.

392
00:20:12.320 --> 00:20:13.720
Was not I promise you.

393
00:20:13.720 --> 00:20:18.120
It's not like just if you don't believe me, OpenAI

394
00:20:18.160 --> 00:20:21.600
put out a white paper talking about how when they moved like

395
00:20:21.680 --> 00:20:25.760
strictly people who are one shotting tasks from working in an interactive session

396
00:20:25.760 --> 00:20:30.400
where they did not interact at all, to going through tickets and PRs.

397
00:20:30.440 --> 00:20:34.200
They saw five x productivity increase over the course of like 2 to 3 months.

398
00:20:34.200 --> 00:20:38.320
So a lot of the reason this works is because once you start working

399
00:20:38.320 --> 00:20:41.480
in this way, one is it forces you to start getting it

400
00:20:41.480 --> 00:20:45.000
right up front because you can't make quick course corrections.

401
00:20:45.000 --> 00:20:48.080
You can't say, oh, wait, no, don't delete the database, right?

402
00:20:48.120 --> 00:20:50.440
Like you just like if you didn't get it right, it's deleted.

403
00:20:50.440 --> 00:20:53.800
They know it won't delete the database, but also

404
00:20:53.840 --> 00:20:57.200
it puts everything that you interact with the agent in a legible surface.

405
00:20:57.200 --> 00:21:01.320
So you have your tickets and then you have PRs with comments.

406
00:21:01.640 --> 00:21:05.320
It now is very easy to just point

407
00:21:05.360 --> 00:21:09.120
another agent at that and say, hey, fix all the comments from the last week.

408
00:21:09.160 --> 00:21:13.560
Like any recurring tasks or any recurring feedback I've given the agent,

409
00:21:13.960 --> 00:21:16.200
find some improvement to the code base that would fix it.

410
00:21:16.200 --> 00:21:20.160
Put up a PR so it makes the harness engineering loop easier.

411
00:21:20.160 --> 00:21:21.920
But most importantly, remember how I said it

412
00:21:21.920 --> 00:21:25.280
sucks to have to revert the change and try again?

413
00:21:25.720 --> 00:21:26.920
You don't have to anymore.

414
00:21:26.920 --> 00:21:29.360
You just leave comments on the PR,

415
00:21:29.360 --> 00:21:32.320
let it fix it, and then have another process

416
00:21:32.320 --> 00:21:34.240
that comes in in the background and cleans up

417
00:21:34.240 --> 00:21:36.880
and does the harness engineering on your behalf.

418
00:21:36.880 --> 00:21:38.880
Or you can do it yourself, but at least you can pick up

419
00:21:38.880 --> 00:21:40.200
all of the comments that you left.

420
00:21:40.200 --> 00:21:45.720
So this seems like a small thing, but it really is the most crucial starting

421
00:21:45.720 --> 00:21:49.600
unlock to leveling up and working towards more automation.

422
00:21:50.320 --> 00:21:53.840
So once you've got your issue tracker, once you've got PR review

423
00:21:53.840 --> 00:21:57.520
as the main interfaces where you steer and correct

424
00:21:57.520 --> 00:22:00.800
agent work, you're going to start developing lots of workflows.

425
00:22:00.800 --> 00:22:05.160
These will be things like Playbook for adding a feature to the CLI.

426
00:22:05.240 --> 00:22:08.480
It will be features around like how to do

